OpenTV — notice on the engine data
==================================

The code in this repository is OpenTV's own work and is MIT licensed; see
LICENSE.

The data under `data/` is not.

What it is
----------

`data/en/engine.tvdata` holds the constant tables the American English
TruVoice engine reads: its letter-to-sound rules, its exception dictionary
and suffix tables, the phoneme inventory and the per-phoneme formant,
duration and amplitude tables, the prosody and duration-by-rate tables, and
the parameters that define the ten voices. About 760 KB of it.

It is lifted byte for byte out of Centigram's CGRM_EN.DLL by
`tools/extract_data.py`, because the engine's arithmetic depends on the
exact values — the decompilation is verified by producing audio identical to
the original's down to the sample, and that is only meaningful against the
original's own numbers. The tables carry their symbol names from this
project's analysis, but the bytes are Centigram's.

No code is taken. The `.text` section is not copied, apart from fifty-one
jump tables the compiler left sitting between functions, each cut to the
size a C declaration in `src/` gives it. The engine's logic in `src/` is
written from analysis, not lifted.

Whose work it is
----------------

The data is the work of Centigram Communications Corporation, whose
copyright notice the binaries carry as "Copyright (c) 1991-1997 Centigram
Communications Corp."

Where the rights sit now has not been established by this project, and the
trail is not short. What is on the public record:

* June 1997 -- Centigram sold the TruVoice text-to-speech technology and
  business to Lernout & Hauspie Speech Products of Belgium, months before
  the October 1997 build this project works from was even linked.
* October 2001 -- Lernout & Hauspie went bankrupt after the accounting
  fraud uncovered in 2000, and ScanSoft bought the bulk of its assets at
  auction, the text-to-speech and speech-recognition engines among them.
* 2005 -- ScanSoft acquired Nuance Communications and took its name.

Nuance's own later history is not traced here.

The MIT licence does not cover the contents of `data/`, and OpenTV is in no
position to license them to anyone. They are included so that the engine
can be built and studied without hunting down a 1997 DLL, and for no other
reason.

If you hold these rights and would rather the data were not distributed
here, please open an issue on the repository and it will be removed.

The original binaries
---------------------

No Centigram binary is in this repository, and none is needed to build or
run OpenTV.

A copy is needed for one thing only: `tools/difftest.py`, which runs the
corpus through the original DLL and through OpenTV and requires the audio to
be identical. That comparison is the evidence the decompilation is correct.
Without a copy the test reports that it has nothing to compare against and
everything else still works.

`tools/extract_data.py` is how `data/` was produced, and is kept in the tree
so that anyone with the original can check that what is committed here is
what comes out of it.
